# K1 - Entity Trap vs the real structure. Kata sandbox sketch (plan section 3.6).
# [K1-nn] sketch IDs are recognised by validate_architecture / trace and ignored by the
# CI adl-coverage gate. Never [TR-*]; promotion is decided only at K12 (architecture/REVIEW.md).
DESCRIPTION K1 - total-recall's structure partition vs the Entity Trap: vacuous rules and unused allow-lists
CATEGORY Static Coupling
NAMESPACE TrK1NS
DEFINE SYSTEM Total Recall K1 Sandbox AS trk1
    DEFINE COMPONENT Structure Partition AS partition
    DEFINE COMPONENT Allow-lists AS allowlists

# The pp. 23-24 pitfall (a rule whose strings matched nothing, so it passed while checking zero
# classes) applied to our partition: the REVERSE direction of TR-S12. TR-S12 proves every file has
# one owner; nothing proves every owner still has a file. A component that owns no code (after a
# rename, or a stale ADL line) makes every rule about it vacuously green.
ASSERT(Structure Partition has no missing link: every component declared in the production structure.adl has exactly one file-set in components.ts, and every file-set still owns at least one production file) # [K1-01]

# TR-S6 and TR-S11 are allow-lists (a ceiling), not claims that an edge exists. An allow-list whose
# allowed targets are never imported by its owner restricts nothing worth restricting. The
# existence reading (IS DEPENDENT ON: at least one import), never a per-file rule:
ASSERT(Each allow-list rule in the production structure.adl is exercised: its owner imports at least one of the targets it allows) # [K1-02]

# Not re-asserted, answered with existing evidence (answers.md): junk drawers and orphan nodes
# -> TR-S12; enumerated leaves -> TR-S1; the single definition site of the state singleton -> TR-S15.
